RYAN WEBER: Welcome
to Cloud OnAir,

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.230
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.640
We are hosting
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:24.424
My name is Ryan Weber, Project
Manager of Google Sheets.

00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:26.340
And today, I'll be talking
about Google Sheets

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.550
and giving you some tips and
tricks to boost productivity.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.900
You can ask questions at
any time on the platform.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.890
And we have Googlers
standing by to answer them.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.390
So with that, let's get started.

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:43.330
All right.

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:45.340
So today, we'll be
covering tips to boost

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:48.940
productivity that will span the
typical analytical workflow.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.360
Now, the first stage in
this analytical workflow

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:52.450
is the collect phase.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:55.240
This is to get data from
wherever it may live and bring

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.940
it right into your spreadsheets.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.800
Next, we have the prepare flow.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.020
This is where you the
data in your Sheets

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.569
and you clean it and get
it ready for analysis.

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:08.860
And for the next flow, the
analyze and visualize stage.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.825
Now, in this stage,
you're taking the data

00:01:10.825 --> 00:01:12.700
and you're actually
generating those insights

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.640
and those learnings to
help figure out and make

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.066
sense of that data.

00:01:18.066 --> 00:01:19.440
Finally, we have
the share phase.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.320
This is where you're
sharing out your data

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:23.960
in your spreadsheet
in order to get,

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.540
either your collaborators in
the spreadsheet to help you,

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:29.120
or to share with end
recipients who will be viewing

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.620
and consuming that content.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.830
Now, all of this is on top
of real-time collaboration.

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:38.040
This is at the heart of not only
Sheets, but of G Suite itself.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.456
And we'll go into a bit more
about how to get the most out

00:01:40.456 --> 00:01:42.894
of the collaboration.

00:01:42.894 --> 00:01:45.060
So that's the order of
topics we'll be going through

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.530
in today's talk.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:49.160
And we'll end it with
talking about how Sheets

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:50.970
is ready for
Enterprise so you can

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:55.480
get the most of spreadsheets
in your company.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:56.700
So with that, let's dive in.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:01.180
Let's talk about getting
data into your spreadsheet.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:03.400
So first, let's talk
about templates.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.252
So templates have
been added to Sheets.

00:02:06.252 --> 00:02:07.960
We have dozens of
professionally-designed

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.395
templates to choose from.

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:11.020
They cover a wide
variety of use cases.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:12.760
And rather than
starting from scratch,

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:14.590
you can open up a
template and start

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:18.149
entering data using an
already crafted template.

00:02:18.149 --> 00:02:20.440
Additionally, you can create
your own domain templates.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.270
These are templates
that are specific

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:23.710
to your particular company.

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:25.960
Let's say, for example,
that your finance team

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:29.114
has a particular way they like
to report out every month.

00:02:29.114 --> 00:02:31.030
Rather than recreating
that report every month

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:34.090
from scratch, you can create
a template once, save it

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:35.470
to the Template Gallery.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.060
And that way,
within your company,

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:38.830
that domain template
is available for you

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:41.890
with all the themes,
colors, font, et cetera,

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.957
that your company uses
already in the spreadsheet.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.300
Next, you can get data into your
spreadsheets by importing it.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:52.770
Let's say someone
sends you a file.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:55.200
It could be many
different types of files.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.370
We have support for CSV,
for example, Comma Separated

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:00.630
Value files, in Excel.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:03.330
And right from Gmail, there
will be an attachment card

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:04.710
like you see on the screen.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.090
And you would simply
click the Pencil icon.

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:09.210
It will open up that
file into Sheets.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:11.540
And you can edit
right within Sheets

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:12.880
with one click-- very simple.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.900
You also can get data into
Sheets by import functions.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.085
So we have a whole host
of import functions

00:03:22.085 --> 00:03:24.210
that you can use when you're
writing your formulas,

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.550
things that will pull
in data from externally

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:31.020
saved files, from RSS feeds,
from web pages, from XML.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.640
But the one that's
used the most often

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.290
is the first one, the
Importrange function.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:38.260
This allows you to pull in
data from other spreadsheets.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:41.820
So you can write a formula
that has an import range, put

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:44.419
in the URL of the other formula,
and that will pull in data

00:03:44.419 --> 00:03:45.460
from another spreadsheet.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:47.820
It's an easy way to link
spreadsheets and pull

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:49.559
in data from other sources.

00:03:49.559 --> 00:03:51.350
So I would encourage
you to check that out.

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.370
You don't have to remember all
the information on this slide.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.710
If you just start typing
equal and start typing import,

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:57.110
you'll see autocomplete
will show up along

00:03:57.110 --> 00:03:59.485
with some information that
will help you learn a bit more

00:03:59.485 --> 00:04:03.444
about these functions.

00:04:03.444 --> 00:04:05.680
Google Forms is another
really popular way

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:07.670
to get data into Sheets.

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:09.400
So with Google
Forms, you can simply

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.647
create a series of questions
of all different types.

00:04:12.647 --> 00:04:13.480
You set up the form.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.580
You can send it out
to a bunch of people

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:17.709
from whom you want to
collect that information.

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:19.209
And as they fill
it out and submit,

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:21.094
that data will flow
right into Sheets

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:22.510
where you can do
further analysis,

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:25.300
you can create your charts,
your pivot tables and all that

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:26.380
on that data--

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:26.960
very simple.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.520
Next, you can get data
into Sheets via our API.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:38.410
So APIs allow third parties
to integrate with Sheets.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:43.270
So you can get data both from
Sheets into third party sources

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:46.240
as well as from third party
sources right into Sheets.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:48.580
And we worked hard
to continue to update

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:50.920
the API to support all the
functionality in Sheets.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:52.628
As we add new features,
we're continually

00:04:52.628 --> 00:04:55.084
adding more capabilities
into the API.

00:04:55.084 --> 00:04:56.500
One thing we've
added in last year

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.305
is called Developer metadata.

00:04:58.305 --> 00:04:59.680
What that allows
developers to do

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:03.350
is to stay on top of how
users interact with Sheets.

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:07.280
So you have data within a sheet
that's linked to a CRM system,

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:08.280
for example.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.330
And the users in the
sheet are rearranging

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:12.880
columns, or renaming
things, or changing rows.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:15.580
The metadata allows developers
to stay on top of those changes

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.450
so that everything
is still in sync.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:21.410
So that's a pretty
powerful feature.

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:24.030
Next, we're excited to
talk about SAP to Sheets.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.580
This is a new feature that
launched in the last year.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.910
Now we have seamless export
from SAP into Sheets.

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:32.512
Now we know a lot of
companies live on SAP.

00:05:32.512 --> 00:05:34.220
All the operations
they have stored there

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:36.120
is very critical for business.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:37.580
And so now with--

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:41.280
very simple, you can export
from SAP right into Sheets,

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:44.030
open up the file in Sheets,
and see all the data,

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:47.042
and do simple
analysis in Sheets.

00:05:47.042 --> 00:05:48.750
If you want to check
out more about this,

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.810
you can go to the link
on the screen there,

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:53.210
cloud.google.com/sap/sap
and gsuite.

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:02.560
We're also excited to
announce the Sheets data

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:03.670
connectors for BigQuery.

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:05.503
Now, this is actually
something that we just

00:06:05.503 --> 00:06:09.060
announced a month ago at our
Google Cloud Next conference.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.626
This is available
today in a beta.

00:06:11.626 --> 00:06:13.000
And what this
allows you to do is

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.300
connect to BigQuery and to
pull in data from BigQuery

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:17.290
right into Sheets.

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:20.774
So you can write a simple
SQL query in Sheets.

00:06:20.774 --> 00:06:22.190
So if you see on
the screen there,

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:24.100
you can pick your
billing project.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:25.660
You hit write query.

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:27.760
You can enter in some SQL.

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:31.150
And then, when you run your
SQL, if you hit Insert Results,

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:32.840
it'll now run that
query, pull the data,

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:34.510
and pop it into Sheets.

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:37.170
What that does now is, if
you want to refresh the data,

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:38.590
all you need to do is
hit the Refresh button,

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:40.480
and it will refresh the
data for you automatically.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:42.130
Then they can share with
their collaborators.

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:44.254
They also can refresh that
data if they have access

00:06:44.254 --> 00:06:45.670
to the underlying data source.

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:47.711
No need for them to write
additional SQL queries.

00:06:47.711 --> 00:06:49.424
It's very simple to
have that connection.

00:06:49.424 --> 00:06:51.840
If you're interested in trying
out this feature in limited

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:54.100
beta, go to that
link on the screen,

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:57.740
gsuite.google.com/bq-sheets,
and sign up for the beta.

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:04.960
I'm also excited to talk about
a data connector to Salesforce.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:08.410
So with Salesforce,
we now have an add-on

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:11.140
that was created by Google
Cloud in beta program.

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:14.830
You can now pull in data
from Salesforce into Sheets,

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:16.870
and you also can push
changes from Sheets

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:18.302
back into Salesforce.

00:07:18.302 --> 00:07:20.260
So for those of you who
are interested in that,

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:21.176
please check that out.

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:25.230
All right.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:27.438
That's a few different ways
to get data from wherever

00:07:27.438 --> 00:07:28.677
it may live into Sheets.

00:07:28.677 --> 00:07:30.510
Now, let's talk about
transforming that data

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:32.020
and getting it
ready for analysis.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:33.860
So in order to prep
your data, there's

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:35.320
a few things you can do.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:36.966
First, we have Text to columns.

00:07:36.966 --> 00:07:38.340
So if you have
data that you want

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.780
to turn into a table
of columns, you

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:42.772
can use Text to columns feature.

00:07:42.772 --> 00:07:45.230
We've had support for that for
a while on things like comma

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:48.180
separating values,
tabs, et cetera.

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:49.830
So that's all been
there for a while.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:50.905
You can use that today.

00:07:50.905 --> 00:07:52.530
And we've also added
support for what's

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:54.510
called fixed-width formatting.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:56.250
So the file on the
screen on the left

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:58.922
is an example of
fixed-width formatted file.

00:07:58.922 --> 00:08:00.880
And if you import it now
using Text to columns,

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:03.270
it will automatically be
turned into the tabular

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:04.240
data on the right.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:06.900
So again, Text to columns is
how you can transform raw data

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:08.310
into a table of data.

00:08:10.639 --> 00:08:12.430
If you're preparing
your data for analysis,

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:15.520
you also might want to be
expanding and collapsing

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:16.330
rows and columns.

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:18.750
This is a common Enterprise
request we've had.

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:21.200
And we've added it in the
last couple of months.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.122
So now you can easily collapse
and expand columns and rows

00:08:25.122 --> 00:08:26.830
with ease by using
this Grouping feature.

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:32.794
We've also introduced
Checkboxes into Sheets.

00:08:32.794 --> 00:08:34.419
So what Checkboxes
will allow you to do

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:36.720
is, if you have a column,
and in that column

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:38.380
you have two values--

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:40.860
true/false, yes/no,
reviewed/unreviewed--

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:42.279
whatever it might be--

00:08:42.279 --> 00:08:44.070
you can transform it
into a checkbox, where

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:46.950
one value will be checked, and
one value will be unchecked.

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:51.090
Then you can treat the checkbox
like any other value in Sheets.

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:53.370
So if you want to use it
in a formula, you can.

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:54.882
If you want a filter
on it, you can.

00:08:54.882 --> 00:08:56.340
You can use
conditional formatting.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:59.154
The underlying values
are still there.

00:08:59.154 --> 00:09:00.570
But it just looks
like a checkbox,

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:02.611
which we've found users
are really excited about.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:07.670
We've also introduced macros.

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:10.671
This is a huge
productivity booster.

00:09:10.671 --> 00:09:13.170
It can save you a lot of time
by automating repetitive work.

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:16.940
And so what macros are
doing is, you hit Record,

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:19.910
and you can do a bunch of
stuff in Sheets, whether it's

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:22.280
formatting data, creating
a chart, whatever you

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:24.380
might be doing,
you are recording

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:25.940
that using the Macro Recorder.

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:27.520
And then, when you
hit Save, it will

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:29.090
remember everything you did.

00:09:29.090 --> 00:09:31.790
So then you can go back
and replay that macro,

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:33.800
and it will repeat
all the steps for you.

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:35.687
That way, it'll save you time.

00:09:35.687 --> 00:09:37.520
It will prevent you
from missing a step when

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:39.070
you need to repeat it.

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:41.012
So it's a big
productivity booster.

00:09:41.012 --> 00:09:43.220
I definitely encourage you
to check that out as well.

00:09:45.544 --> 00:09:47.210
Sheets also has what
are called Add-ons.

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:49.910
So add-ons are a way to add
additional functionality

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:50.900
into Sheets.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:52.400
So when it comes
to data prep, there

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.110
are a bunch of
add-ons that you can

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:57.010
use to enhance your Sheets--
things like removing

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:59.420
duplicates, merging
data add-ons,

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:01.730
normalizing values, detecting
outliers, et cetera.

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:03.730
And so all that, again,
will enhance your Sheets

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:05.519
if you add it into
your spreadsheet.

00:10:05.519 --> 00:10:07.310
There's also add-ons
of other types outside

00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:09.726
of data prep-- so things like
Mail [? Merge, ?] et cetera.

00:10:09.726 --> 00:10:12.360
But if you're interested
in supercharging

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:14.960
your spreadsheets, definitely
check out the Add-ons store

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:17.990
and find some great
add-ons that you can use.

00:10:21.299 --> 00:10:23.340
Next, I'm going to talk
about Google Apps Script.

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:24.880
So Google Apps
Script-- what is it?

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:28.150
It's a scripting language
in the Google Apps platform.

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:28.775
What can it do?

00:10:28.775 --> 00:10:29.899
It can do a bunch of stuff.

00:10:29.899 --> 00:10:31.360
One, you can create automation.

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:34.270
So when you are
actually recording

00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:36.370
a macro, what it's doing
underneath the covers

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:39.549
is actually writing those
steps in Apps Script.

00:10:39.549 --> 00:10:41.090
You also can manually
do that as well

00:10:41.090 --> 00:10:44.387
if you want to get
more complex than what

00:10:44.387 --> 00:10:45.470
you're trying to automate.

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:48.160
So you can create those
automations with Apps Script.

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:50.110
You also can write
custom functions.

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:52.920
So Sheets gives you over 400
functions out of the box.

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:54.640
But let's say you
have a new function

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:55.681
that you've come up with.

00:10:55.681 --> 00:10:57.550
You can create it
using Apps Script

00:10:57.550 --> 00:11:00.050
and then use it like any other
function within your formulas

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:01.625
in your spreadsheets.

00:11:01.625 --> 00:11:03.750
And finally, you can use
Apps Script with triggers.

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:07.900
So triggers are when there's
actions that happen in Sheets.

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:10.630
So when someone opens
a file, when someone

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:13.120
makes an edit to a file-- all
those are triggers that then

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:15.280
can kick off some
of the automations

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:17.510
you created in Apps Script.

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:21.400
So that's another powerful
way to enhance what's

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:22.891
possible in your spreadsheet.

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:27.810
So with Apps Scripts-- you
can see that on the right--

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:30.930
it's basically JavaScript with
additional capabilities added

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:32.050
to it.

00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:34.201
And we've worked hard to
make it fully featured.

00:11:34.201 --> 00:11:36.450
And so this year, we've added
additional functionality

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:38.989
like pivot tables, charts,
the conditional formatting,

00:11:38.989 --> 00:11:40.530
among other things,
into Apps Script.

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:44.040
So if you've tried it
before, give it another shot,

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:47.254
and see what's possible now.

00:11:47.254 --> 00:11:48.670
So with that, I'm
going to jump in

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:51.800
to show you a demo of how
to use some of this stuff.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:55.250
So let's take a
look at a file here.

00:11:55.250 --> 00:11:57.750
So this file has four tabs.

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:00.152
Each tab is quarterly data.

00:12:00.152 --> 00:12:01.610
The data is different
in every tab,

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:03.151
but the columns are
all the same-- so

00:12:03.151 --> 00:12:04.454
all the same structure.

00:12:04.454 --> 00:12:06.370
So what I like to do is
I like to clean it up.

00:12:06.370 --> 00:12:08.180
I like to prepare it just once.

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:10.970
I'll do it on this Q1 tab
and then replay those changes

00:12:10.970 --> 00:12:12.350
on subsequent tabs.

00:12:12.350 --> 00:12:15.740
So to do that, I can go to
the Tools Menu, go to Macros,

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:17.760
and hit Record Macro.

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:20.510
What this is going to do is,
now you see a blinking red light

00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:21.240
at the bottom.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:21.830
So now, it's listening.

00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:23.330
It's recording what
I'm going to do.

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:28.080
So every action I take now,
it will track those actions.

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:29.830
And so what I can
do is if I-- let's

00:12:29.830 --> 00:12:33.860
say I want to highlight all of
the data in the spreadsheet.

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:35.120
I can do that.

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:37.966
And I want to resize
it now to fit.

00:12:37.966 --> 00:12:39.590
You'll see that, next
to the red light,

00:12:39.590 --> 00:12:42.549
it's capturing all of those
actions as I'm recording it.

00:12:42.549 --> 00:12:44.090
So let's add some
alternating colors.

00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:47.290
We'll change it to green.

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:48.440
Let's be done with that.

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:53.809
And we also can freeze the first
row so that's easy to scroll.

00:12:53.809 --> 00:12:55.850
And then there's a bunch
of true and false values

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:57.310
in that column.

00:12:57.310 --> 00:12:59.690
Let's see, how about let's
change this to percentage.

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:02.310
And we can fix the
precision to be--

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:03.402
yep, that's better.

00:13:03.402 --> 00:13:04.860
And then for the
true and false, we

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:06.890
can actually use
checkboxes, rather

00:13:06.890 --> 00:13:08.015
than saying true and false.

00:13:08.015 --> 00:13:09.660
So Data Validation--

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:12.820
I'll change this to
Checkbox and hit Save.

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:15.080
And so now that's been changed.

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:17.206
And then finally, these
columns, Start and Finish--

00:13:17.206 --> 00:13:19.621
I want to hide those columns
so I can add the new grouping

00:13:19.621 --> 00:13:20.410
feature.

00:13:20.410 --> 00:13:24.090
Let's group columns G and H,
and I can collapse this now.

00:13:24.090 --> 00:13:25.380
So this looks good.

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:26.790
Let's say I'm done with this.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:30.440
I feel like this is an
adequately prepared sheet.

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:31.280
I will save it.

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:32.457
I'll give it a name.

00:13:32.457 --> 00:13:34.040
So here, you can
give it whatever name

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:37.000
you want for easy recall later.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:39.890
And then I can give it
a keyboard shortcut,

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:42.120
in this case,
Command-Option-shift-1,

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:43.410
and we'll hit Save.

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:44.660
So now it's saving the macro--

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:46.160
Macro Saved-- great.

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:50.390
So now if I go to Q2, this tab--
rather than redoing everything

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:53.720
manually, I can go to the
Macros Menu, find that macro,

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:54.560
and just replay it.

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:57.615
And it will do all those
steps for me instantly.

00:13:57.615 --> 00:13:59.727
It helps me prevent
making mistakes,

00:13:59.727 --> 00:14:01.310
helps prevent me
from missing a step--

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:04.700
very simple, like magic.

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:06.890
So that's how macros work.

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:08.510
All right.

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:11.790
Moving on to Analyze
and Visualize--

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:13.650
so this is where the
rubber meets the road.

00:14:13.650 --> 00:14:16.190
This is where we are
going to discover

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:20.370
all those insights to get
the most out of our data.

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:22.240
So pivot tables are in Sheets.

00:14:22.240 --> 00:14:23.090
For those of you
who are unfamiliar,

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:25.006
pivot tables are a way
to summarize your data,

00:14:25.006 --> 00:14:29.190
to group and aggregate
data in useful ways.

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:31.075
Sheets has had pivot
tables for a while,

00:14:31.075 --> 00:14:33.200
but we worked hard to make
them even better for you

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:34.197
this past year.

00:14:34.197 --> 00:14:35.780
So this is the old
pivot table format.

00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:38.600
We've actually overhauled them
to look a little nicer like you

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:40.660
see on the screen.

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:42.504
So we think it looks
a little better.

00:14:42.504 --> 00:14:44.420
We've added a bunch of
other features as well.

00:14:44.420 --> 00:14:45.770
We now have drill downs.

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:47.750
This is the ability
to either double click

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:49.940
or say Show Details
on a particular cell

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:51.860
and see all the
underlying values.

00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:52.760
We have groupings.

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:56.330
So you can now group up
arbitrarily on values and also

00:14:56.330 --> 00:14:58.410
expand out in different ways.

00:14:58.410 --> 00:15:00.380
We have Show Value
as a Percentage--

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:02.780
was a common request, as well
as being able to customize

00:15:02.780 --> 00:15:04.227
the text in the headers.

00:15:04.227 --> 00:15:06.560
You can also choose whether
or not to repeat row labels.

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:07.950
And again, as I
mentioned before,

00:15:07.950 --> 00:15:09.110
we have the Refreshed UI.

00:15:09.110 --> 00:15:11.430
So all that has made
pivot tables even better.

00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:13.290
So again, I encourage
you to check that out.

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:18.152
It's a great tool
in doing analysis.

00:15:18.152 --> 00:15:19.860
Next, I want to talk
about different ways

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:21.120
to filter data in Sheets.

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:23.370
Sheets actually has several
different ways to do this.

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:25.406
So first are what
are called filters.

00:15:25.406 --> 00:15:27.951
Now filters change
content for everybody.

00:15:27.951 --> 00:15:29.325
So when you filter
down to, let's

00:15:29.325 --> 00:15:31.732
say, a particular
value, anyone else

00:15:31.732 --> 00:15:33.690
doing that spreadsheet
will see the same thing.

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:36.490
They'll all see
the filtered data.

00:15:36.490 --> 00:15:38.245
We also have what are
called Filter views.

00:15:38.245 --> 00:15:40.830
Now, Filter views-- when
you filter down to something

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:44.710
using a Filter view, it's only
doing it for you, the viewer.

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:46.120
It only impacts you.

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:49.320
Anyone else in that spreadsheet
will not see the changes.

00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:51.810
That's the difference
between that and filters.

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:54.680
Just to really
hit this home, you

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:57.052
should use filters when you
want to purposely change

00:15:57.052 --> 00:15:59.010
the view for everybody
viewing the spreadsheet.

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:01.860
If you want to impact
everybody, use filters.

00:16:01.860 --> 00:16:04.450
Also, any changes you make while
using filters-- for example,

00:16:04.450 --> 00:16:05.790
changing the sorting--

00:16:05.790 --> 00:16:09.805
that will impact everybody even
after you removed the filter.

00:16:09.805 --> 00:16:11.610
Now filter views,
you should use those

00:16:11.610 --> 00:16:14.614
when you don't want to disrupt
others' views of the data.

00:16:14.614 --> 00:16:16.530
So again, if you don't
want people to see what

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:18.570
you're doing, use filter views.

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:21.269
You also can save
multiple filter views

00:16:21.269 --> 00:16:22.060
in the spreadsheet.

00:16:22.060 --> 00:16:23.934
So let's say you have
multiple different ways

00:16:23.934 --> 00:16:25.830
of cutting and slicing
and dicing the data.

00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:29.070
You can use filter views
to save all of those.

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:32.310
If you want to share a link
to another user with things

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:34.620
already pre-filtered
you can share a link

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:37.020
to a particular filter
view-- another useful way

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:38.470
to use Filter views.

00:16:38.470 --> 00:16:41.310
And if someone shares
a sheet with you,

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:43.410
and you don't have edit
access, you actually

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:45.380
can't use filters
because that's going

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:46.830
to change the underlying data.

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:49.460
But you can use filter views,
because filter views allow

00:16:49.460 --> 00:16:51.660
even viewers who
don't have edit access

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:53.090
to the filter of the data.

00:16:53.090 --> 00:16:55.590
So again, definitely encourage
you to check out filter views

00:16:55.590 --> 00:16:56.450
if you haven't.

00:16:56.450 --> 00:16:59.128
They're like filters, but
built for collaboration.

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:05.329
Now Sheets has over 400
spreadsheet functions--

00:17:05.329 --> 00:17:07.490
some of them listed
on the right there.

00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:09.920
We have everything you
can probably think of.

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:10.970
We have all the basics--

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:13.729
VLOOKUP, SUMIF,
MATCH, et cetera.

00:17:13.729 --> 00:17:15.520
We've also added a
bunch in the last year--

00:17:15.520 --> 00:17:18.401
some of the more complicated
ones you see on the screen

00:17:18.401 --> 00:17:18.900
there--

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:21.530
statistical
functions, et cetera.

00:17:21.530 --> 00:17:23.172
We've even added
functions that are

00:17:23.172 --> 00:17:25.130
unique to Sheets, things
like AVERAGE.WEIGHTED,

00:17:25.130 --> 00:17:26.660
so that's a weighted average.

00:17:26.660 --> 00:17:28.035
And then we have
a new one called

00:17:28.035 --> 00:17:32.880
SORTN, which actually shows you
the top N values in a range.

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:35.460
Let's go a little deeper on
that one just as an example.

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:37.580
So let's say you want to
find the top five values

00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:39.840
from some Salesforce data.

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:42.530
So in this case, I want
to find the top five

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:44.530
regions by revenue.

00:17:44.530 --> 00:17:47.165
What I can do-- and this is the
results that I'd like to see.

00:17:47.165 --> 00:17:49.970
The way to get that is I
can use the SORTN function,

00:17:49.970 --> 00:17:52.610
specify the range, and say
I want the top five values.

00:17:52.610 --> 00:17:54.771
And voila, you get the
data that you see above.

00:17:54.771 --> 00:17:56.270
Now, if I didn't
have this function,

00:17:56.270 --> 00:17:58.478
it would be a lot of work
to make this happen, pretty

00:17:58.478 --> 00:17:59.519
complicated.

00:17:59.519 --> 00:18:01.810
Luckily, SORTN is there--
makes it much simpler for me.

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:07.590
Sheets also has
Formula suggestions.

00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:09.499
This is another
productivity booster.

00:18:09.499 --> 00:18:11.040
Let's say you're
doing some analysis.

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:12.330
You have some data.

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:16.077
You go below your table
data, and you type Equals.

00:18:16.077 --> 00:18:17.910
All of a sudden, you'll
see some suggestions

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:19.800
of different formulas
that you can use.

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:22.406
So Sheets is predicting what
it thinks you will want.

00:18:22.406 --> 00:18:24.780
You can choose one of those
rather than typing things out

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:26.250
manually.

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:28.250
We found that Suggestions
have made formal entry

00:18:28.250 --> 00:18:30.752
five times faster for
those who are using it.

00:18:30.752 --> 00:18:32.250
It helps speed
things up for you,

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:33.910
helps prevent you
from making mistakes.

00:18:33.910 --> 00:18:35.980
So if you see those popping
up, hopefully, you're

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:38.630
taking advantage of those and
getting that productivity boost

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:39.950
with formula suggestions.

00:18:43.030 --> 00:18:45.310
Next, we'll talk about charts.

00:18:45.310 --> 00:18:48.070
Now, no analysis is complete
without some charts.

00:18:48.070 --> 00:18:50.810
We, at the Sheets
team love our charts.

00:18:50.810 --> 00:18:53.260
We have all the basic
charts that you need.

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:56.230
We have the pie charts,
line charts, scatter plots,

00:18:56.230 --> 00:18:57.499
bar charts.

00:18:57.499 --> 00:18:59.290
We've also introduced
a new chart this year

00:18:59.290 --> 00:19:00.760
called waterfall chart.

00:19:00.760 --> 00:19:02.760
You can see that
on the left there.

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:05.740
Waterfall charts allow you
to see incremental changes

00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:08.125
between different segments.

00:19:08.125 --> 00:19:10.000
We've also introduced
a lot of other features

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:12.458
to charts in the last year--
things like total data labels.

00:19:12.458 --> 00:19:16.000
We've added a way to customize
your colors, tight alignment,

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:18.220
moving data labels,
customizing the line styles,

00:19:18.220 --> 00:19:20.740
and we've revamped
the chart UI to make

00:19:20.740 --> 00:19:22.430
it more user friendly as well.

00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:24.435
So again, play
around with charts

00:19:24.435 --> 00:19:26.810
and see how to get the most
out of visualizing your data.

00:19:30.642 --> 00:19:32.100
Next, I want to
talk about Explore.

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:34.610
So Explore is bringing
intelligence into Sheets.

00:19:34.610 --> 00:19:36.730
You can think about it
as almost like having

00:19:36.730 --> 00:19:40.360
your own personal analyst
built right into Sheets.

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:43.960
So With Explore, you can ask
natural language questions

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:46.810
about your data, and
it'll give you answers.

00:19:46.810 --> 00:19:48.940
Explore will suggest charts.

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:50.100
It can adjust pivot tables.

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:54.279
It can give you formatting
suggestions-- very simple.

00:19:54.279 --> 00:19:56.570
So on the bottom right there,
you see the Explore icon.

00:19:56.570 --> 00:19:57.320
You click on that.

00:19:57.320 --> 00:19:59.330
That will open up
the Explore sidebar.

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:01.900
And you can click on the
alternating color suggestions.

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:04.806
You can ask those natural
language questions very simply.

00:20:04.806 --> 00:20:06.640
Again, it's like
having an analyst right

00:20:06.640 --> 00:20:08.465
within your spreadsheet.

00:20:08.465 --> 00:20:09.840
In the last year,
we've continued

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:12.120
to enhance what's
possible with Explore.

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:13.970
Now, you actually can
use natural language

00:20:13.970 --> 00:20:16.890
to ask for charts
and pivot tables.

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:19.140
And even if you don't know
to ask for those questions,

00:20:19.140 --> 00:20:21.030
it will actually even
suggest pivot tables to you.

00:20:21.030 --> 00:20:22.530
So that's a new
feature that we've also

00:20:22.530 --> 00:20:23.613
launched in the last year.

00:20:26.630 --> 00:20:28.140
So with that, let's do a demo.

00:20:31.330 --> 00:20:33.120
So here, we have
some Salesforce data.

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:35.944
And we can go down
to the Explore icon.

00:20:35.944 --> 00:20:37.110
Click on that to open it up.

00:20:40.460 --> 00:20:43.111
And we see now, the
Explore side bar is open.

00:20:43.111 --> 00:20:45.360
So what I can do is I see a
bunch of suggestions here.

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:46.901
I see some pivot
tables, some scatter

00:20:46.901 --> 00:20:48.750
plots, some different charts.

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:51.510
Let's say I want to ask
a question about my data.

00:20:51.510 --> 00:20:53.680
I can type in this box here.

00:20:53.680 --> 00:20:57.180
So I want the total
revenue by region.

00:20:57.180 --> 00:21:00.270
And voila, I have a pivot table.

00:21:00.270 --> 00:21:03.710
If I want, I also can view
this as a chart as well.

00:21:03.710 --> 00:21:04.920
So I click over here.

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:06.890
There's the chart.

00:21:06.890 --> 00:21:08.677
Back to the pivot table.

00:21:08.677 --> 00:21:11.260
Now you'll notice that I didn't
even use the word pivot table.

00:21:11.260 --> 00:21:12.850
I just said total
revenue by region.

00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:14.830
But yet, it knew that
a way to summarize

00:21:14.830 --> 00:21:16.160
that is to the pivot table.

00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:18.420
So let's now insert
this into your tab.

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:21.580
OK, great.

00:21:21.580 --> 00:21:25.480
Now you'll see that I can
expand this out by date.

00:21:25.480 --> 00:21:27.280
Let's choose Close date.

00:21:27.280 --> 00:21:30.140
And this has added
date as columns for me.

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:31.750
And so I see the
individual days here.

00:21:31.750 --> 00:21:34.442
What I want to do is actually
group them by quarter.

00:21:34.442 --> 00:21:36.400
So now, it's going to
summarize all those dates

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:39.590
by quarter as my columns
against the regions in the rows.

00:21:39.590 --> 00:21:41.260
So I can see all of
my data summarized

00:21:41.260 --> 00:21:43.290
by both region and quarter.

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:44.650
I can click around here.

00:21:44.650 --> 00:21:46.240
And I notice that
in Q2 Southwest

00:21:46.240 --> 00:21:48.236
region is an
unusually high value.

00:21:48.236 --> 00:21:50.110
So I want to dig into
that a little bit more.

00:21:50.110 --> 00:21:51.651
I'll right click,
go to Show Details.

00:21:51.651 --> 00:21:54.340
And now I'm doing a
drill down into the data

00:21:54.340 --> 00:21:55.600
underlying that $5 million.

00:21:58.270 --> 00:22:01.810
So here, you can see all the
values in Q2 corresponding

00:22:01.810 --> 00:22:02.920
to the Southwest region--

00:22:02.920 --> 00:22:04.570
so very simple.

00:22:04.570 --> 00:22:06.880
So that is how to use both
Explore and Pivot Tables.

00:22:10.262 --> 00:22:12.220
So with that, let's jump
into the next section.

00:22:12.220 --> 00:22:14.170
This is share and distribute.

00:22:14.170 --> 00:22:16.110
So again, the reasons
to do this are either A,

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:18.040
you want to share out
to collaborators so they

00:22:18.040 --> 00:22:19.994
can help you refine
your analysis

00:22:19.994 --> 00:22:21.910
and get more out of your
data, or B, maybe you

00:22:21.910 --> 00:22:24.100
want to share to
your leadership team

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:26.080
or to your managers
to show them all

00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:30.550
the great analysis and results
that you found in Sheets.

00:22:30.550 --> 00:22:33.040
So let's talk about the first
use case-- collaborating.

00:22:33.040 --> 00:22:35.610
So Sheets allows everyone
to edit together seamlessly.

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:38.110
Again, real time collaboration
has been there since day one.

00:22:38.110 --> 00:22:40.651
Here, you see a bunch of people
clicking around, interacting,

00:22:40.651 --> 00:22:41.655
changing values.

00:22:41.655 --> 00:22:43.780
It's very simple to work
together in a spreadsheet.

00:22:43.780 --> 00:22:46.690
No more sending
around attachments.

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:49.270
No more worrying about
getting locked out of a file

00:22:49.270 --> 00:22:51.560
or being in an out-of-date file.

00:22:51.560 --> 00:22:54.070
Everyone's going to get the
right file at the same time.

00:22:54.070 --> 00:22:55.020
It's very seamless.

00:22:57.824 --> 00:22:59.490
Next, let's say you
wanted to distribute

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:02.940
your files by putting them
into either Slides or Docs.

00:23:02.940 --> 00:23:05.920
Well now Google
supports embedding.

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:08.820
So with this, you can embed
both charts and tables

00:23:08.820 --> 00:23:10.775
into both slides and docs.

00:23:10.775 --> 00:23:12.150
And when you do
that, it actually

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:14.460
stays linked to the
underlying Sheets file.

00:23:14.460 --> 00:23:17.160
So the Sheets file remains
the single source of truth.

00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:20.961
That way, if later, you change
the data in your spreadsheet,

00:23:20.961 --> 00:23:23.460
you can go back to your Slides
and Docs and, with one click,

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:29.520
refresh those charts and tables
very simply rather than getting

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:32.844
out of date or worrying about
showing the wrong analysis.

00:23:32.844 --> 00:23:34.260
So again, that's
a very simple way

00:23:34.260 --> 00:23:39.360
to keep data up to date all
within the Google Editors.

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:45.739
We've also worked hard to
help you with printing.

00:23:45.739 --> 00:23:47.780
We know a lot of you like
to share and distribute

00:23:47.780 --> 00:23:49.410
via printing.

00:23:49.410 --> 00:23:52.140
And so we've added a bunch of
new features in the last year,

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:56.000
things like custom page breaks,
custom headers and footers,

00:23:56.000 --> 00:23:58.535
custom paper sizes, print
preview, custom margins,

00:23:58.535 --> 00:23:59.720
and saving print settings.

00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:01.090
So all of that is there.

00:24:01.090 --> 00:24:03.260
For those of you who like
to print, check that out.

00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:08.856
Another huge productivity
booster we find--

00:24:08.856 --> 00:24:11.480
and we use all the time here at
Google, in fact-- are Comments.

00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:15.670
So Comments are a rich,
discussion-style functionality

00:24:15.670 --> 00:24:17.110
built right into Sheets.

00:24:17.110 --> 00:24:19.480
You can simply click on
a cell, add a comment.

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:21.910
If you Plus mention
someone-- so you

00:24:21.910 --> 00:24:23.560
put a plus sign and
an email address,

00:24:23.560 --> 00:24:25.390
or it at sign and
an email address,

00:24:25.390 --> 00:24:27.554
it will autocomplete
your contact's name.

00:24:27.554 --> 00:24:29.470
They'll get an email
notification telling them

00:24:29.470 --> 00:24:31.720
that they were
mentioned in a sheet.

00:24:31.720 --> 00:24:33.280
They click in that email.

00:24:33.280 --> 00:24:34.900
It'll bring them
right to the file--

00:24:34.900 --> 00:24:38.180
to the right file, to the
right tab, to the right cell--

00:24:38.180 --> 00:24:40.300
where they can either
take action on the cell

00:24:40.300 --> 00:24:43.180
directly, or they can reply
to the comment itself.

00:24:43.180 --> 00:24:46.150
So you can have a threaded
conversation between you

00:24:46.150 --> 00:24:47.210
and your collaborators.

00:24:47.210 --> 00:24:48.251
It's very useful.

00:24:48.251 --> 00:24:50.000
We also have what are
called Action items.

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:52.360
These are a special kind of
comment, where you actually

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:54.310
can assign a particular
cell to someone.

00:24:54.310 --> 00:24:56.680
So let's say you need
someone to review a number

00:24:56.680 --> 00:24:58.552
or fill out a cell.

00:24:58.552 --> 00:25:00.760
Rather than just passively
mentioning them as an FYI,

00:25:00.760 --> 00:25:04.800
you can say, hey, Sarah, can
you please take care of this.

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:09.400
And so she'll be assigned that
cell so that it'll get done.

00:25:11.957 --> 00:25:13.790
Next, when you're
collaborating with people,

00:25:13.790 --> 00:25:16.790
sometimes it's useful to
know who has seen that file.

00:25:16.790 --> 00:25:18.550
So we have now
Activity Dashboard--

00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:20.496
another new feature in Sheets.

00:25:20.496 --> 00:25:22.370
So let's say you share
it out with your team.

00:25:22.370 --> 00:25:24.530
It's been a few days,
and you're curious--

00:25:24.530 --> 00:25:26.270
OK, well, who has
actually reviewed

00:25:26.270 --> 00:25:27.819
it, and who hasn't yet?

00:25:27.819 --> 00:25:30.110
Well, the Activity Dashboard--
it'll show you all that.

00:25:30.110 --> 00:25:31.859
And then from this
dashboard, you actually

00:25:31.859 --> 00:25:34.670
can email the various people
you've collaborated with.

00:25:34.670 --> 00:25:36.740
So you can find those who
haven't reviewed it yet

00:25:36.740 --> 00:25:38.156
and email them
from this interface

00:25:38.156 --> 00:25:40.040
and say, hey,
please take a look.

00:25:40.040 --> 00:25:43.580
I need answers to
this by next Tuesday.

00:25:43.580 --> 00:25:46.400
So again, if that is a use
case that resonates with you,

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:50.330
check out Activity Dashboard.

00:25:50.330 --> 00:25:52.990
Sheets has something
called Version History.

00:25:52.990 --> 00:25:54.504
This is very powerful.

00:25:54.504 --> 00:25:56.170
All the edits that
are created in Sheets

00:25:56.170 --> 00:25:57.514
are saved within Sheets.

00:25:57.514 --> 00:25:58.930
And so in Version
History, you can

00:25:58.930 --> 00:26:03.140
see who's made changes,
what have they changed,

00:26:03.140 --> 00:26:04.590
and when they made
those changes.

00:26:04.590 --> 00:26:06.423
So all that is available
to you as an editor

00:26:06.423 --> 00:26:08.320
of the spreadsheet.

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:10.570
We also have what are
called Named Versions.

00:26:10.570 --> 00:26:12.700
So Named Versions
actually allow you

00:26:12.700 --> 00:26:15.070
to save a name,
or multiple names,

00:26:15.070 --> 00:26:17.350
to different
important milestones

00:26:17.350 --> 00:26:19.720
in your spreadsheet's history.

00:26:19.720 --> 00:26:22.120
So let's say you have a
couple of different drafts

00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:24.190
you want to mark as
important milestones,

00:26:24.190 --> 00:26:27.660
or you have a final
version that was read out

00:26:27.660 --> 00:26:31.249
to your leadership review, you
can keep track of all of that.

00:26:31.249 --> 00:26:33.040
And you can use this
now to actually create

00:26:33.040 --> 00:26:34.570
what's called a
Diff, or see what's

00:26:34.570 --> 00:26:37.922
changed between adjacent
important milestones.

00:26:37.922 --> 00:26:39.130
That's called Named Versions.

00:26:39.130 --> 00:26:39.963
Also check that out.

00:26:43.340 --> 00:26:45.645
Sheets also has
Notification rules.

00:26:45.645 --> 00:26:47.270
So Notification rules
are useful if you

00:26:47.270 --> 00:26:50.820
want to be notified as soon
as there are edits being made.

00:26:50.820 --> 00:26:53.192
So let's say you have a
bunch of collaborators,

00:26:53.192 --> 00:26:54.650
and you want to
stay on top of when

00:26:54.650 --> 00:26:55.910
they're making those edits.

00:26:55.910 --> 00:26:57.285
Using Notification
rules, you can

00:26:57.285 --> 00:26:59.750
say email me every
time a change is

00:26:59.750 --> 00:27:03.311
made, either when the
change happens, or email me

00:27:03.311 --> 00:27:05.060
a digest, or a summary,
of all the changes

00:27:05.060 --> 00:27:06.267
at the end of the day.

00:27:06.267 --> 00:27:07.850
So again, this is a
more proactive way

00:27:07.850 --> 00:27:10.599
of staying on top of all those
changes in your spreadsheet.

00:27:14.231 --> 00:27:14.730
Now, great.

00:27:14.730 --> 00:27:16.380
We've gone through
a lot of features

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:17.610
in the analytical workflow.

00:27:17.610 --> 00:27:19.320
We want to talk now
about how Sheets

00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:22.320
is helping users get even
more out of their spreadsheets

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:24.910
for business.

00:27:24.910 --> 00:27:27.931
So one, Sheets works hard to
keep your data safe and secure.

00:27:27.931 --> 00:27:29.430
No longer do you
need to send around

00:27:29.430 --> 00:27:31.500
attachments that could
get easily downloaded

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:32.640
or put onto a USB drive.

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:35.150
Everything is in files
that require you just

00:27:35.150 --> 00:27:36.245
to authenticate.

00:27:36.245 --> 00:27:38.370
In fact, you can also use
two-factor authentication

00:27:38.370 --> 00:27:41.370
for even extra added security.

00:27:41.370 --> 00:27:43.650
Also recently announced is
Confidential Mode in Gmail

00:27:43.650 --> 00:27:47.050
where the text
itself in the email

00:27:47.050 --> 00:27:50.920
is locked down and harder to
distribute beyond that email

00:27:50.920 --> 00:27:51.420
itself.

00:27:51.420 --> 00:27:54.999
It's harder to forward,
copy, or print, et cetera.

00:27:54.999 --> 00:27:56.790
So all of that is
working hard to make sure

00:27:56.790 --> 00:28:00.011
that your data and your
company's stay safe and secure.

00:28:00.011 --> 00:28:02.260
We also have something called
Prevent download, print,

00:28:02.260 --> 00:28:03.130
and copy.

00:28:03.130 --> 00:28:05.310
So if you are the
owner of a file--

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:07.530
you're the one who
create that file--

00:28:07.530 --> 00:28:09.840
you have the ability in
the Advanced Sharing dialog

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:13.680
to check the Disable options
to download, print, and copy

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:14.610
feature.

00:28:14.610 --> 00:28:17.010
Now, what that will do is,
for commenters and viewers,

00:28:17.010 --> 00:28:19.920
they won't be able to do those
items in that file any longer.

00:28:19.920 --> 00:28:21.780
So again, it's a
way to help prevent

00:28:21.780 --> 00:28:24.060
accidental leaking
of data, people

00:28:24.060 --> 00:28:26.200
accidentally printing
out a very sensitive file

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:32.210
and having it go beyond
where it was intended to go.

00:28:32.210 --> 00:28:34.950
You also can set
expirations on Sheets files.

00:28:34.950 --> 00:28:37.670
So let's say you
have someone you

00:28:37.670 --> 00:28:39.770
want to share with who
only needs limited access

00:28:39.770 --> 00:28:41.030
a limited amount of time.

00:28:41.030 --> 00:28:44.910
What you can do is you can find
that file, you can go to share,

00:28:44.910 --> 00:28:47.405
you can pop in that
person's email address.

00:28:47.405 --> 00:28:49.280
And what you can do is
you go to the Advanced

00:28:49.280 --> 00:28:51.560
version of dialogue,
and you share with them,

00:28:51.560 --> 00:28:52.880
there's a little clock.

00:28:52.880 --> 00:28:53.930
If you click on
that clock, you can

00:28:53.930 --> 00:28:56.430
choose that you only want to
share with them for seven days.

00:28:56.430 --> 00:28:57.405
And you hit Save.

00:28:57.405 --> 00:28:59.930
What that's done is that's
now said that for seven days,

00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:00.530
they'll have access.

00:29:00.530 --> 00:29:01.370
But at the end of
the seventh day,

00:29:01.370 --> 00:29:03.520
they'll automatically
lose access to that file.

00:29:03.520 --> 00:29:05.353
Again, that prevents
them from having access

00:29:05.353 --> 00:29:08.870
to files that are more
sensitive for a longer time

00:29:08.870 --> 00:29:09.620
than you intended.

00:29:13.410 --> 00:29:18.420
Additionally, we know that
sometimes folks in business

00:29:18.420 --> 00:29:21.360
like to travel.

00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:23.550
And so when you're
traveling and you're

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:26.730
on a plane with spotty Wi-Fi or
no Wi-Fi at all, don't worry.

00:29:26.730 --> 00:29:28.320
Sheets works both
online and offline.

00:29:28.320 --> 00:29:29.750
So we've got your back--

00:29:29.750 --> 00:29:33.295
will work no matter how good
your Wi-Fi connectivity is.

00:29:33.295 --> 00:29:36.010
Speaking of traveling, we
also work across your devices.

00:29:36.010 --> 00:29:38.640
Whether you're on your
laptop, tablet, or mobile,

00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:40.604
we have a Sheets
experience there

00:29:40.604 --> 00:29:43.020
across different devices that
you can stay productive even

00:29:43.020 --> 00:29:43.520
on the go.

00:29:46.459 --> 00:29:48.500
Finally, we know how
important keyboard shortcuts

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:49.170
are for folks.

00:29:49.170 --> 00:29:52.340
And so we've worked
to try and provide

00:29:52.340 --> 00:29:55.820
abilities to help transition
to Sheets by making it easier

00:29:55.820 --> 00:29:58.350
to mirror some of the
shortcuts in Sheets with those

00:29:58.350 --> 00:30:00.980
that you might be used
to in other programs.

00:30:00.980 --> 00:30:02.450
So there's now a
switch that will

00:30:02.450 --> 00:30:05.830
allow you to override some
of the browser shortcuts

00:30:05.830 --> 00:30:08.300
that were originally claimed
by browsers like Chrome

00:30:08.300 --> 00:30:12.320
and Firefox to now match
what you might be expecting

00:30:12.320 --> 00:30:14.160
the spreadsheet behavior to be.

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:17.015
And so if you are someone who
loves their keyboard shortcuts,

00:30:17.015 --> 00:30:17.640
check that out.

00:30:20.951 --> 00:30:21.450
All right.

00:30:21.450 --> 00:30:24.580
So that was a lot of different
tips and tricks there.

00:30:24.580 --> 00:30:27.270
So now I'm going to
do a brief recap.

00:30:27.270 --> 00:30:29.020
I won't read through
all these items here.

00:30:29.020 --> 00:30:31.350
But going over the
high-level buckets-- again,

00:30:31.350 --> 00:30:33.350
we talked about how to
get data into Sheets

00:30:33.350 --> 00:30:35.100
and all the different
ways you can do that

00:30:35.100 --> 00:30:38.730
through importing from your
Gmail, using Google Forms,

00:30:38.730 --> 00:30:40.530
through the APIs, et cetera.

00:30:40.530 --> 00:30:42.310
We talked a bit about
Preparing your data.

00:30:42.310 --> 00:30:44.585
So again, this is you can
do things like splitting out

00:30:44.585 --> 00:30:47.280
your columns using text to
columns, you can use macros,

00:30:47.280 --> 00:30:49.110
you can use Apps
Script, et cetera.

00:30:49.110 --> 00:30:52.560
In the analyze phase, we talked
about pivot tables, checkboxes,

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:54.525
charts, formulas,
and using Explore

00:30:54.525 --> 00:30:57.230
so you can bring
intelligence to your Sheets.

00:30:57.230 --> 00:30:59.580
In sharing and
distribution, we talked

00:30:59.580 --> 00:31:02.520
about all the ways you can both
share with your collaborators,

00:31:02.520 --> 00:31:06.540
as well as using things like
embedding, using notification

00:31:06.540 --> 00:31:08.460
rules, and Version
History to really get

00:31:08.460 --> 00:31:09.817
the most out of collaboration.

00:31:09.817 --> 00:31:11.400
And finally, we
talked a bit about how

00:31:11.400 --> 00:31:12.930
Sheets is Enterprise-ready.

00:31:12.930 --> 00:31:15.829
So we talked about how
you can take advantage

00:31:15.829 --> 00:31:18.120
of using links, expirations,
preventing copy, printing,

00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:20.830
download, et cetera, to
really protect your data.

00:31:23.610 --> 00:31:26.930
All right, so that concludes
the main part of this talk.

00:31:26.930 --> 00:31:29.689
Stay tuned for live Q&amp;A.
We'll be back in a minute.

00:32:35.494 --> 00:32:36.090
All right.

00:32:36.090 --> 00:32:36.700
Welcome back.

00:32:36.700 --> 00:32:38.930
We're now going to do
a couple of questions

00:32:38.930 --> 00:32:41.350
so Let's jump over to
questions from the audience.

00:32:41.350 --> 00:32:42.540
First question.

00:32:42.540 --> 00:32:45.570
"I'm starting to run into
the 2 million cell limit.

00:32:45.570 --> 00:32:48.300
Is that Sheets team
planning on raising this?"

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:49.620
I've got good news for you.

00:32:49.620 --> 00:32:50.240
We are.

00:32:50.240 --> 00:32:51.948
So actually, we just
announced that we'll

00:32:51.948 --> 00:32:54.840
be raising it from 2
million to 5 million cells

00:32:54.840 --> 00:32:58.620
We hope that that will
help further increase

00:32:58.620 --> 00:33:00.960
the size of the files that
you're able to use today.

00:33:00.960 --> 00:33:03.270
And we'll continue
looking at ways in order

00:33:03.270 --> 00:33:08.820
to further expand that, as well
as making Sheets at any level

00:33:08.820 --> 00:33:10.050
faster and more performant.

00:33:10.050 --> 00:33:11.760
We know it's important
for you to have

00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:13.775
Sheets that run quickly.

00:33:13.775 --> 00:33:16.150
And so we're constantly looking
at ways to optimize that.

00:33:16.150 --> 00:33:17.119
So the answer is yes.

00:33:17.119 --> 00:33:19.410
2 million will become 5
million, and we're also looking

00:33:19.410 --> 00:33:23.160
at other ways to speed it up.

00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:24.580
Next question.

00:33:24.580 --> 00:33:27.220
"Are there any plans
to improve charts?"

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:27.769
Yes.

00:33:27.769 --> 00:33:29.810
So we've done a lot with
charts in the last year.

00:33:29.810 --> 00:33:31.660
I mentioned the waterfall charts
and a bunch of other features

00:33:31.660 --> 00:33:33.702
that we've added to
charts in the last year.

00:33:33.702 --> 00:33:34.910
But we're not stopping there.

00:33:34.910 --> 00:33:36.993
We're going to continue
to make them even better--

00:33:36.993 --> 00:33:39.730
common requests around
customization of charts,

00:33:39.730 --> 00:33:45.276
ability to fine tune and tweak
what's possible with charts.

00:33:45.276 --> 00:33:46.900
One of things that
we're working on now

00:33:46.900 --> 00:33:50.950
is actually the ability to
update individual points

00:33:50.950 --> 00:33:52.630
or bars on individual charts.

00:33:52.630 --> 00:33:54.310
So stay tuned for that.

00:33:54.310 --> 00:33:56.635
That will be one
example of customization

00:33:56.635 --> 00:34:00.730
that, hopefully, will make
charts even better for you.

00:34:00.730 --> 00:34:02.260
Next question.

00:34:02.260 --> 00:34:04.660
"This presentation showed
us a lot about what

00:34:04.660 --> 00:34:06.250
you can do in Sheets today.

00:34:06.250 --> 00:34:09.639
Where is Sheets headed
over the next year or two?"

00:34:09.639 --> 00:34:10.900
That's a good question.

00:34:10.900 --> 00:34:14.620
So we've been thinking a lot
about how to further improve

00:34:14.620 --> 00:34:16.250
what's possible with analysis.

00:34:16.250 --> 00:34:17.739
And we're thinking
a lot about how

00:34:17.739 --> 00:34:20.866
to take advantage of, again,
data that lives elsewhere--

00:34:20.866 --> 00:34:23.199
even especially large data
sets-- and how you can really

00:34:23.199 --> 00:34:25.070
analyze that in Sheets.

00:34:25.070 --> 00:34:29.139
We talked a bit about
the BigQuery integration

00:34:29.139 --> 00:34:29.980
with Sheets.

00:34:29.980 --> 00:34:31.749
That's in beta today.

00:34:31.749 --> 00:34:33.290
That's just the
first of what we hope

00:34:33.290 --> 00:34:35.061
will be several
different features

00:34:35.061 --> 00:34:37.060
and functionality in
Sheets where you can really

00:34:37.060 --> 00:34:39.550
do really rich and
robust analysis

00:34:39.550 --> 00:34:40.690
on your external datasets.

00:34:40.690 --> 00:34:42.760
So keep an eye out
for all of that.

00:34:42.760 --> 00:34:46.155
We hope you're excited, as
we are, about all of what's

00:34:46.155 --> 00:34:46.780
possible there.

00:34:49.449 --> 00:34:50.440
Next question.

00:34:50.440 --> 00:34:52.949
"What's the best approach
for transitioning from Excel

00:34:52.949 --> 00:34:53.670
to Sheets?

00:34:53.670 --> 00:34:55.659
Have you seen companies
do this well?"

00:34:55.659 --> 00:34:57.330
It's a common
question that we get.

00:34:57.330 --> 00:35:02.670
We know that many of you are
coming from the Excel world.

00:35:02.670 --> 00:35:06.432
And so this can be a
challenge, and we admit that.

00:35:06.432 --> 00:35:08.390
One thing we recommend
is you don't necessarily

00:35:08.390 --> 00:35:09.900
have to do it all at once.

00:35:09.900 --> 00:35:13.410
A lot of companies will
phase in certain segments

00:35:13.410 --> 00:35:14.580
of their company.

00:35:14.580 --> 00:35:17.440
So for example, maybe you start
off with marketing or sales.

00:35:17.440 --> 00:35:19.320
It's often easier for
them to transition

00:35:19.320 --> 00:35:22.425
immediately than those who
use longer tail functionality

00:35:22.425 --> 00:35:23.910
in Sheets, like
the finance team.

00:35:23.910 --> 00:35:26.460
But we really think everyone
can transition into Sheets

00:35:26.460 --> 00:35:28.717
over time.

00:35:28.717 --> 00:35:30.300
So it's just a matter
of them learning

00:35:30.300 --> 00:35:32.465
where the things that they
know and love in Excel, where

00:35:32.465 --> 00:35:33.340
that lives in Sheets.

00:35:33.340 --> 00:35:35.460
So again, there are a lot
of common misconceptions

00:35:35.460 --> 00:35:37.200
about-- oh, Sheets doesn't
have this function,

00:35:37.200 --> 00:35:38.200
it doesn't pivot tables.

00:35:38.200 --> 00:35:38.847
Well, we do.

00:35:38.847 --> 00:35:40.430
They just have to
learn where that is.

00:35:40.430 --> 00:35:43.230
And so there's a lot of great
help content in our Learning

00:35:43.230 --> 00:35:45.840
Center that help provide
transition guides

00:35:45.840 --> 00:35:48.120
and help users learn how to
make those transition more

00:35:48.120 --> 00:35:49.070
seamlessly.

00:35:49.070 --> 00:35:54.680
So hopefully, even your
most stubborn finance user

00:35:54.680 --> 00:35:57.560
will hopefully find what he
or she needs to do their work

00:35:57.560 --> 00:36:01.830
and can move over
without too much pain.

00:36:01.830 --> 00:36:02.530
Last question.

00:36:02.530 --> 00:36:04.470
"What's your favorite
spreadsheet function?"

00:36:04.470 --> 00:36:06.979
So I almost said VLOOKUP.

00:36:06.979 --> 00:36:07.770
That's a great one.

00:36:07.770 --> 00:36:11.480
But I think actually my favorite
is probably the QUERY function.

00:36:11.480 --> 00:36:13.890
This is one that I think
is unique to Sheets.

00:36:13.890 --> 00:36:14.850
It's very powerful.

00:36:14.850 --> 00:36:17.930
Actually, you can
use a type of SQL

00:36:17.930 --> 00:36:19.920
right from within
a formula to do

00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:23.430
some really rich
selection and filtering

00:36:23.430 --> 00:36:25.060
and querying of your data.

00:36:25.060 --> 00:36:27.412
So if you haven't seen
the QUERY function,

00:36:27.412 --> 00:36:29.120
definitely encourage
you to try that out.

00:36:29.120 --> 00:36:30.720
I think for you power
users out there,

00:36:30.720 --> 00:36:33.670
you'll really like
what you see, I think.

00:36:33.670 --> 00:36:39.120
And with that, I think that
concludes today's webinar.

00:36:39.120 --> 00:36:42.420
Thanks so much for your time.

00:36:42.420 --> 00:36:45.770
[MUSIC PLAYING]

